In the early 1900s, the British palaeontologist Clive Forster Cooper dug in the Bugti Hills of Balochistan, then part of British India and now in Pakistan. He found bones of an enormous animal and named it Paraceratherium in 1911.

Soon other giant bones turned up. Forster Cooper named a second set from Balochistan Baluchitherium in 1913. A Russian scientist studied bones from Kazakhstan and called them Indricotherium. In 1922, the American Roy Chapman Andrews led an [[expedition|expedition]] to Mongolia that found a huge skull. For decades, books used all three names, and many old dinosaur books show "Baluchitherium" towering over the other beasts. Most experts now place them in one group, with Paraceratherium as the oldest name.

