The Samrat Yantra, or "supreme instrument", is a huge [[@pythagoras|right-angled triangle]] of stone with a staircase up its sloping side. The slope points at the [[@north-star|North Star]] and is parallel to Earth's [[axis|axis]]. Curved scales on each side catch the shadow.

As the Earth turns, the shadow sweeps across the scales at about 1 mm a second. Stand beside it and you can watch it creep, a few centimetres every minute. Marks on the scales let readers tell local time to within a few seconds, more precisely than most [[@clocks|clocks]] of the age.

{{story:jaipur}}

In 1978, a NASA scientist named Donald Kessler warned of a possible chain reaction. If there is enough debris in one orbit, crashes make more debris, which causes more crashes, and so on. In the worst case, some orbits could become too dangerous to use for many years. This is called the Kessler syndrome.

Scientists disagree about how close we are to that point. Most agree that the number of pieces is growing, and that it would be wise to clean up before it gets worse. Like [[@ocean-plastic|plastic in the ocean]], it is much easier to stop adding junk than to clear it up afterwards.

{{story:ocean-plastic}}

In laboratories, tuns have survived being cooled to about −272°C, just one degree above absolute zero, the coldest anything can get. They have lived through short heating to about 150°C, hotter than boiling oil in a kadhai.

They have survived pressure about six times greater than at the bottom of the Mariana Trench, the deepest point of the ocean. Some [[species|species]] shrug off radiation about 1,000 times stronger than what would kill a human. One has a protein, named Dsup, that seems to shield its DNA like an umbrella.

{{story:space-animals}}

In 1889 Paris unveiled the [[@eiffel-tower|Eiffel Tower]], 300 metres of wrought iron. Many artists hated it and called it ugly. It held the height record until New York's Chrysler Building passed it in 1930, followed a year later by the Empire State Building.

Steel frames changed everything. Instead of thick walls holding up the weight, a skeleton of steel beams does the work. Add electric lifts, and suddenly people could live and work hundreds of metres in the air.

{{story:lifts-skyscraper-speed}}

Stepwells were meeting places. Women came to fill pots and to talk. Travellers on trade routes stopped to drink and rest. Many stepwells had small shrines, and people prayed there, because water was sacred as well as precious.

That is why so many are beautifully carved. Builders used the same skills they used in [[@architecture-temples|temple architecture]]: elephants, dancers, flowers and gods were cut into the stone walls. Going down the steps could feel like walking into a hidden temple.

{{story:unesco-sites}}

The race begins more than a year before the vote. Candidates from each party compete in contests called primaries and caucuses, state by state, to win their party's support.

In the summer, each party holds a big convention and officially names its candidate. Then come months of rallies, adverts and televised debates. Polls try to measure who is ahead, often in [[@percentages|percentages]] that change from week to week.

{{story:george-washington}}

The Reserve Bank of India began on 1 April 1935. It prints rupee notes, sets interest rates and watches over other banks. In 1969, the government nationalised 14 large banks, taking them into public ownership, partly to open more branches in villages.

For a long time, many Indians had no bank account at all. In 2014, the government launched the Jan Dhan Yojana to open free basic accounts, and more than 50 crore have been opened since. Today, many Indians move money in seconds using [[@upi-payments|UPI]], which links bank accounts to mobile phones.

{{story:upi-payments}}

Louis Braille lost his sight after an accident when he was three. At a school for blind children in Paris, books used big raised letters that were slow to read and heavy to carry. A former army captain had shown the school a code of raised dots meant for soldiers to read at night.

Braille, aged about 15, made it far simpler. His system used just six dots, arranged like the six on a domino, to stand for every letter. That was in 1824. Today [[@braille|braille]] appears on lift buttons, medicine boxes and railway signs, and blind readers can feel a whole word with one fingertip.

{{story:braille}}

When India's [[@constitution|Constitution]] was written in the late 1940s, millions of Dalits were still barred from temples, wells and schools. B. R. Ambedkar, who chaired the drafting committee and was himself born into a Dalit family, argued that freedom meant little without a fair share of power and opportunity.

So the Constitution allowed the state to make "special provision" for these groups. Seats in the Lok Sabha and state assemblies were reserved for Scheduled Castes and Scheduled Tribes, at first for ten years. [[parliament|Parliament]] has extended that time limit again and again ever since.

{{story:ambedkar}}

Many countries elect their president this way, including Brazil, France, Indonesia, Argentina and Nigeria. In Brazil, where voting is compulsory for most adult [[citizen|citizens]], the runoff is held on the last Sunday of October, four weeks after the first round. In 2026, Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva won a runoff to become Brazil's president for a fourth time.

Some places use a quicker version called instant runoff. Voters rank the candidates 1, 2, 3 on one ballot. If nobody has a majority, the last-placed candidate is removed and their voters' next choices are counted, until someone passes 50%. Australia uses this for many elections.

{{story:vote-how}}

Your body clock affects much more than sleep. Your body temperature is usually lowest in the early hours before dawn and highest in the late afternoon or evening. Many people feel a dip in energy in the early afternoon, the time when a short [[@naps|nap]] feels most tempting. Your [[digestion|digestion]], blood pressure and alertness all follow daily patterns.

This is why the time of day matters. Many athletes perform best in the late afternoon, and doctors are studying whether some medicines work better at certain hours.

{{story:plants-move}}

Your bones work as [[@lever-lift|levers]], and your joints are the pivot points. Most muscles attach close to a joint, which means they must pull very hard to move a weight held far away. This set-up is good for speed, and less good for strength.

Body shape matters too. Lifters with short arms find the bench press easier, because the bar travels a short distance. Lifters with long arms often find the deadlift easier. Champions often have body shapes that suit their sport. Strongmen are often very tall and heavy, while Olympic lifters in the lighter classes are short and compact, with arms and legs that move the bar a shorter distance.

{{story:extreme-strongest-animals}}

In the 1800s, the British wanted maps of Tibet, but foreigners were not allowed in. So the Survey of India trained Himalayan men to go instead, disguised as traders or pilgrims. They were called the Pundits.

The most famous was Nain Singh Rawat, a schoolteacher from Kumaon. He trained himself to take steps of the same length, uphill or down. He counted them on a string of 100 beads, not the normal 108, and hid notes in a prayer wheel. In 1866 he reached Lhasa and worked out its height above sea level by checking the temperature at which water boiled. His careful counts helped put Tibet on British [[@maps-history|maps]].

{{story:cipher-codes}}

In 1952, Prime Minister [[@nehru|Jawaharlal Nehru]] visited Alappuzha. Local people arranged a race of snake boats in his honour. A boat called Nadubhagam Chundan won. Nehru was so thrilled that, despite security worries, he jumped aboard the winning boat.

Back in Delhi, he sent a silver trophy shaped like a snake boat, and it has been the prize ever since. Since 1955 the race has been held on Punnamada Lake. Today it takes place on the second Saturday of August, over a course of about 1,370 metres, and dozens of boats of different kinds take part.

{{story:rhythm-beat}}

The cliffs began as molten rock that cooled slowly deep underground millions of years ago. Over time the land rose and the softer rock on top wore away, leaving the hard granite exposed.

Rivers cut a narrow canyon through it. Then, during the ice ages, [[@glaciers|glaciers]] crawled down the canyon and ground its sides smooth and steep. When the ice melted, it left the U-shaped valley we see today, with streams pouring over the edges as waterfalls. Half Dome, the rounded rock that looks sliced in two, got its flat face partly from rock peeling away in giant sheets.

{{story:glaciers}}

In the markets and army camps around Delhi, from about the 1200s onwards, Khari Boli mixed with Persian, the language of the royal courts. The result was a lively common language often called Hindustani. Over time it split into two written styles. [[@urdu|Urdu]] uses a Persian-Arabic script and more Persian and Arabic words. Hindi uses Devanagari and more words drawn from Sanskrit.

In everyday speech, the two are so close that a Hindi speaker and an Urdu speaker usually understand each other easily. The writer [[@premchand|Premchand]] wrote his stories in both. Many Hindi [[@film-songs|film songs]] use words that belong equally to the two.

{{story:bollywood}}

His most famous play tells of King Dushyanta, who falls in love with Shakuntala, a young woman raised in a forest hermitage. He marries her and gives her his ring, then returns to his palace. A curse makes him forget her, and only the sight of the ring can bring back his memory. Shakuntala loses it while bathing in a river.

The ring turns up inside a fish. The king remembers, and after much sorrow the family is reunited. Their son Bharata, the story goes, gives his name to India's ancient name, Bharat. Kalidasa took the plot from a much shorter version in the [[@mahabharata|Mahabharata]] and turned it into a full drama.

